Z||000006||b lo 0||07-31-2000||11:23 AM||||Is it just me or does EVERYONE act like they know EVERYTHING about Fieros and you don't?  I have people swear to me that "oh, yeah that thing will blow up on you in about 4,000 miles.  Yep" (I have a V6 with 147,000 miles, original engine) or how about "Those things are death traps" like just because the body panels are plastic it's unsafe?  I don't see anyone going up to Corvette drivers saying "Man, that thing is all fiberglass.  You're gonna die in that thing".  Someone actually told me that yesterday!!  Is it just me???<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">  <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||RiceCooker||07-31-2000||02:37 PM||||Nope, it happens to me all the time||||reg||1
Z||000008||BellaFiero||07-31-2000||03:02 PM||||When I first purchased my Fiero, back in the day ( 1997 ), I was warned by the original owner that my friends and family would all warn me against driving it over 100,000 miles. The car was at 90,000 ( I've put that little counter up to 147,500 and I'm still in one piece  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) and he told me not to worry. And he was right. Besides the generic maintenance, alternator, belts, fixing the AC, headlights, fuel pump, throttle body,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> the money pit... er... the car has held up very nicely. She's a real beauty. My friends all warned me that the engines give out at 147,000, and that Pontiac has set them up to discharge at that mileage, thus forcing you to buy a new engine from them. A massive money-making conspiracy. Uh---- yeah. Right. I was warned that I'd die the first time I got into a collision, the first time someone smokes around my car it'll light the panels up like gasoline, that the car will explode if you slam the doors to hard and the gas rocks up against the hot engine.... uh..... okkkaaaayyy.... I was warned that semi's won't see me and will drive right over me, that the first hard turn I take the whole car will flip... etc. etc. etc.<BR>Fieros are the best bang for the buck on the market of any car, yesterday or tomorrow. I love these cars. I don't think they are 'little' - hell, my coupe is as big as a Fox body Stang… maybe not as tall, but the low-slung sleek look only helps to emphasize that it IS a sports car. I have never driven such a comfortable-yet-containing, flexible all-around car. The Fiero serves it's role as a commuter car as well as a sport's car. I would never want a Corvette - it's not a sport's car anymore, it's a boat. I test drove one and fell like I was in a Caddy. Don't get me wrong - IT WAS AWESOME! You get so many looks, and the raw power that you feel translating through the car is beautiful. But it's not what I want. No way. I like the nimble and quick feel of the Fiero, the ability to toss it around a curve and feel totally confident. And besides, I get alot MORE looks in the Fiero. I've discovered that not many people know or remember the Fiero, and being mistaken for a Lamborghini roadster is a daily occurence for me. The Rice Boys just hate me, I pull up next to their car with the $4,000 body kit and I get more looks at my Aero-styled Fiero that set me back under $500 from the wonderful Fiero salvage community ( my Bella started out an 88 base coupe ). <BR>Sorry about the long post, but I just want to affirm my belief that the Fiero is an A-1, tip top sports car and that those rumors and lies that run rampant about it are so much smoke. I swear, the Rice Boy community must console itself with the thought that the cars that roast them at the lights will blow up as soon as the mileage hits 100,000 - never mind that most Fieros are TWICE that by now!!!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||rockcrawl||07-31-2000||07:20 PM||||I just picked up an '86 SE 4cyl this past weekend. It was lightly toasted. It got me thinking about what I can do to keep mine from burning up. Does anyone know exactly what the causes were and what was done by GM to prevent fires? I've only seen burned dukes, but I'm told the 2.8s burn also. I've heard so many different stories, I don't know what to believe. <P>The popularity of the Saturn line really pisses me off, knowing that the technology was taken from the Fiero. It seems like all the auto manufacturers are going to composite panels. Why is it people think they're gonna die in a Fiero, but a Saturn is so safe. I guess I just have to learn to ignore people like that.<BR><P>------------------<BR>'86 GT 3800<BR>'87 GT<BR>Rossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||7
Z||000010||BlackGT||08-01-2000||01:27 PM||||Don`t worry folks....I hear the same BS all the time! I especially like the folks who refer to Fieros as "Matches on Wheels" I crack up every time I hear that then show them what this little match can do when I <BR>roast the tires from a lite and pull away <BR>like a bat outta hell! The real funny stuff<BR>happened when I hit some debris on the road<BR>that sliced a hole in my rear brake line on <BR>the driver`s side......I was slowing down from about 80mph when I noticed smoke coming<BR>from the rear wheel well on that side, then <BR>lost my brakes shortly after that. I managed<BR>to pull off an exit and into a service station away from the pumps..when I did a small fire errupted on that side! Scared the<BR>hell out of me but put it out quickly with an<BR>extinguisher from the attendant and then heard a crack from another un informed motorist about the dangers of Fieros. I calmly explained to him that the source of<BR>the fire was hot brake fluid being squirted <BR>onto a hot caliper and brake fluid being flamable will ignite! Can happen to any car...... haven`t you seen auto racing before and watched a car with a brake problem burst into flames? These cars have a lot of miles and years and brake lines were<BR>not an item I changed initially but since then I have had new calipers installed and <BR>when I can stop buying new toys I`ll install<BR>steel braided lines. Right now I can safely<BR>haul the car down from 125mph at a pretty respectable rate and have had ZERO problems<BR>with the car since! My baby is a daily driver(When I`m not on my bike) and I don`t baby it on the road.....it`s a sports car and I drive it like one. I for one laugh everytime<BR>someone says something negative about the Fiero....tell`em they`re just jealous!! ||||reg||6
